      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Archaeologists began uncovering Ostia Antica in the 19th century, revealing two‑thirds of the Roman town and its role as a grain storage hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Ostia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Punic Wars, Ostia functioned as Rome’s principal fleet base on Italy’s west coast, anchoring the empire’s naval operations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Ostia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 20th‑century football club Ostiamare, founded in 1945, rose through the ranks partly thanks to AS Roma’s scouting during Dino Viola’s presidency.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AS_Ostia_Mare_Lido_Calcio",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After the empire, the Tiber’s shifting course and sea erosion turned Ostia into a marshy, malarial area, prompting its abandonment for centuries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitostiaantica.org/en/history-of-ostia/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1915, Giulio Magni designed the Regina Pacis church, while Marcello Piacentini’s Maritime Hospice, still visible on Paolo Toscanelli Promenade, opened in 1916.",
